# {{Australian}} (also "foot pad") An unmade, minor walking trail formed only by foot traffic.
#*'''1878''', [http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article61831799 "MELBOURNE TO GIPPSLAND." Gippsland Times (Vic. : 1861 - 1954) 12 Apr 1878: 3 Edition: Morning. Web. 23 Feb 2013]
#*:Six miles from Brandy Creek selection ceases, and then there is (to a '''foot-pad''') a long dreary stretch of sandy country extending nearly to the Moe.
#*'''1933''',{{cite news |url=http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article37840079 |title=NEMARLUCK BADLY WOUNDED. |newspaper=[[Western_Mail_(Western_Australia)|Western Mail (Perth, WA : 1885 - 1954)]] |location=Perth, WA |date=26 October 1933 |accessdate=23 February 2013 |page=31 |publisher=National Library of Australia}}
#*:Nemarluck, if wounded in the way described by the aborigines at Talc Head, will keep to the beaten '''footpad''' leading from Delissaville to the Finnis River, and in his weakened state, will not camp any night far from a waterhole or without a fire.
#*'''1950''', [http://nla.gov.au/nla.news-article23020171 "Bush Tracks For Motorists", The Argus (Melbourne), 22 December, p. 29 Supplement: The Argus Week-End Magazine] 
#*:The rough dray track that leads to the south-cast soon becomes a poorly defined '''footpad''' which follows the tortuous pattern of the main spur through to Mount Everard, and then away to east and south to the far corner of the forest
#*'''2008''', John Chapman & Monica Chapman, ''Overland Track'' ISBN 978 1 920995 05 8 p56
#*:Ignore the minor '''foot pad''' to the left — it is a scrubby track leading to a climbers' site known as Geryon Campsite.
#*'''2010?''', [http://www.mthotham.com.au/mountain/summer/bushwalking_trails/ Mt Hotham Bushwalking and Trails]
#*:This AAWT track varies from a four wheel drive track along the Barry Mountains to a '''foot pad''' across the snow grass plains of the high country from Hotham to Mt Bogong.
